    "content": "The Stations of the Cross on Good Friday is a14-step devotion retracing Jesus’ final steps to his crucifixion. Often held in churches or outdoor processions, it allows participants to meditate on Christ's passion and death. Many Catholics pray these stations on Good Friday, often gathering at 3 PM or in the evening to mark the Passion. The 14 Stations of the Cross Jesus is condemned to death Jesus takes up his cross Jesus falls the first time Jesus meets his mother, Mary Simon of Cyrene helps to carry the cross Veronica wipes the face of Jesus Jesus falls the second time Jesus meets the women of Jerusalem Jesus falls the third time Jesus is stripped of his clothing Jesus is nailed to the cross Jesus dies on the cross Jesus is taken down from the cross Jesus is laid in the tomb Key Good Friday Traditions Papal Procession: In Rome, the Pope traditionally leads the Way of the Cross at the Colosseum, with the 2026 Good Friday marking a rare occasion where the Pope carried the cross for all 14 stations. Parish Services: Many churches hold processions at 1:30 PM, 3 PM (the hour of Jesus' death), or in the evening. Devotional Acts: Worshippers move between wooden markers or images in the church, meditating on each scene. Significance: It is a key moment during the Paschal Triduum, focusing on Christ's sacrifice. Paschal Triduum Note: For official prayers, you can refer to the USCCB's Scriptural Stations of the Cross. USCCB's Scriptural Stations of the Cross",
